{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3df","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c88a-6674-7861-9d70-d3e79e98c472"},"sequence":74,"recorded_at":1780531400000143,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"995ec337-5e4d-4fd4-a64e-ae914055ec56","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"995ec337-5e4d-4fd4-a64e-ae914055ec56","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"995ec337-5e4d-4fd4-a64e-ae914055ec56"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c88a-9c06-7942-8d0b-1c9ec222bf21"},"task_id":"01a0c88a-9c06-7942-8d0b-1c9ec222bf21","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c88a-9c06-7942-8d0b-1c9ec222bf21","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev6.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev5.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 5 was reviewed by RUN-260922-f7f2b5 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **two P1**, two P2, one P3. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev5.md first.\n3|**Real progress, stated plainly so you keep it.** The identity-binding P1 is closed — the wrapper decision and the executed restore now bind session, bootstrap, instance and backend generation. The refresh P1 is closed. And the importer outcome grid is finally the right artefact and the right shape: the reviewer reran your fixed-corpus driver unchanged on both trees and got **193 shared keys, zero moved, zero base-only, seven declared candidate-only**, genuinely keyed on `(package, entry, input)` with outcomes and refusals as values. That closes a defect that took four rounds. Keep every bit of it.\n4|\n5|1. **P1-A — the closure-authority invariant has now failed at a THIRD boundary, and that is the finding.** Round 4 it was the missing state document; round 5 the stale caller source state and the failed stop; now `lifecycle.go:558-559` returns an integrity failure when the outcome cannot be installed **after** the engine has already committed input closure and its receipt, and `ops.go:174-183` admits a writable attach because the report scan finds no closure — it cannot distinguish *no closure* from *closure committed, report write failed*. `TestReviewOutcomeWriteFailureMustNotReopen` drives a successful attach, then a quiesce with an `AfterStage` failure scoped to `/outcomes/`: both `lock-session` and `detach-client` execute, quiesce returns `terminal_backend_integrity_failure at quiesce outcome image`, and after the fault is removed a fresh attach succeeds with `InputAuthorized:true` and a vector without `-r`. It fails twice including under `-race`, with no production source mutated. Row 79 claims a lost barrier write fails closed; B34 discloses only a re-observed timestamp after a receipt/report crash, not that input can reopen before retry.\n6|   **Three boundaries in three rounds is a structural answer, not three bugs.** The barrier is being reconstructed from whichever storage artefact happens to have survived, so every new write path is a new hole. Do not add a fourth guard. Either give closure a single authoritative source that admission consults — where the failure to create the proof is never itself permission, and pending-versus-committed closure is representable — or, if that needs the landed receipt owner's contract, **name that owner and the scoped change and hand off a reviewable slice**. The reviewer wrote the same sentence independently. I will accept a revision whose P1-A resolution is \"this requires <owner> to expose <contract>, here is the boundary and here is everything else finished\" as a complete revision 6.\n7|2. **P1-B — an in-flight attach can commit after a successful quiescence.** `ops.go:94` reads the barrier once, `ops.go:100` obtains server admission, and `ops.go:113` then creates the durable writable client receipt without revalidating closure. `TestReviewAttachMustRecheckClosureBeforeReceipt` runs `Execute(attach)` in a goroutine with its ServerAdmission adapter paused on a channel, completes `Execute(quiesce-input)` including its durable report on the main goroutine, then releases the attestation call: attach returns success with writable authorization and a vector without `-r`. It fails twice under `-race` **with no data-race diagnostic** — this is a logical check/use interval, not unsynchronized memory. Make closure and writable-client admission obey one ordering contract at the effect boundary, and include the returned-vector handoff in that contract. Moving the same check later only relocates the interval; the reviewer says so explicitly and will test the new position.\n8|3. **P2-A — three read-error clauses have no witness.** Four single-site admitting narrowings and one applied control were run against the full committed suite with `-count=2`; three survived both repetitions: EISDIR reading the incarnation document becoming absence (`Execute(attach)` → `checkAttachQuiesced` → `LookupIncarnation`), ENOTDIR reading the outcomes directory becoming absence, and EISDIR reading one outcome document being skipped (both through `Execute(attach)` → `QuiesceBarrierProven`). R3 is worth reading: it stages a `.json` symlink to a directory so `DirEntry.IsDir` does not skip it and the production `ReadFile` returns EISDIR. The fourth plant — the unsafe-root refusal bypassed **only for attach** — was killed, but note why the reviewer built it: your rows mutate the create, status and restore wiring on a shared gate and leave attach unattacked. Ship the three missing production-entry tests and their narrowing rows; do not infer read-error coverage from the existing identity and empty-key tests.\n9|4. **P2-B — your three exclusions from the outcome corpus are false, and the reviewer disproved them by calling the entries.** You excluded `cloneproject`, `sshtransport` and `crashgate` for having \"no exported pure callable surface\". `cloneproject.Normalize` has deterministic pre-effect no-fetcher and no-sink refusals; `sshtransport.New(config.Snapshot{})` refuses an invalid snapshot without starting SSH or touching the network; `crashgate.Lookup` is a pure known/unknown boundary lookup. A five-row supplemental driver called all three on both trees, exited 0 twice and recorded identical outcomes. Put representative `(package, entry, input) -> outcome` rows for these three into your corpus so it covers 36 of 36, and keep any genuinely effectful-success bounds with an accurate reason rather than a blanket one.\n10|5. **P3 — recount the census denominator and fix the remaining prose**, item by item as the verdict lists them. Recount mechanically; do not restate.\n11|\n12|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint; base `d4bd91d0`, and if trunk moves refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the closure-authority design decision from item 1 (implemented, or escalated with the owner named), the ordering contract from item 2, and the extended corpus; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 6 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n13|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n14|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n15|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n16|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n17|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n18|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n19|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n20|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
